import datetime, os

# Rotate through original short-form history/science mysteries by UTC day of year.
stories = [
("The Mary Celeste", "In 1872, the ship Mary Celeste was found drifting in the Atlantic with its crew missing. Much of its cargo remained, and there was no clear sign of a struggle. The lifeboat was gone. Investigators proposed several explanations, but none has been proven. The crew's fate remains one of maritime history's enduring mysteries.", "Mary Celeste ship;historic sailing ship;Atlantic Ocean historical map"),
("The Voynich Manuscript", "The Voynich Manuscript is a centuries-old illustrated book written in a script that has resisted convincing translation. Its pages contain unfamiliar plants, diagrams, and strange scenes. Researchers have suggested ciphers and invented languages, but no interpretation has won broad acceptance. The book is real; what its text means remains unresolved.", "Voynich manuscript;medieval manuscript botanical illustration;medieval manuscript"),
("The Antikythera Mechanism", "A Greek shipwreck yielded a corroded device packed with interlocking gears. Studies revealed that it could model astronomical cycles and predict celestial events. Built more than two thousand years ago, it is often described as an ancient mechanical calculator. Its sophistication changed historians' understanding of technology in the ancient world.", "Antikythera mechanism;ancient Greek shipwreck;ancient Greek astronomy"),
("The Nazca Lines", "Across Peru's Nazca Desert, enormous lines and figures were made by removing dark surface stones to reveal lighter ground. Some form animals and geometric patterns best seen from above. Archaeologists have linked them to ritual landscapes and water-related beliefs, but their exact purposes are still debated.", "Nazca Lines Peru;Nazca geoglyph hummingbird;Peru desert aerial"),
("The Dyatlov Pass Incident", "In 1959, nine hikers died during an expedition in Russia's Ural Mountains. Their abandoned campsite sparked decades of speculation. Modern investigations support natural explanations involving severe weather and a possible snow slab, though details remain debated. The case shows how incomplete evidence can turn tragedy into legend.", "Dyatlov Pass Ural Mountains;Ural Mountains winter;Soviet hiking expedition archive"),
("The Franklin Expedition", "In 1845, two British ships sailed into the Arctic seeking the Northwest Passage. Neither returned. Later searches found abandoned equipment and records, and the wrecks were located much later. Evidence points to harsh ice, starvation, and illness, but many details of the final months remain uncertain.", "HMS Erebus Arctic expedition;Franklin expedition historical map;Arctic ice ship"),
("The Tunguska Event", "In 1908, a huge explosion flattened trees across a remote region of Siberia, yet investigators found no confirmed impact crater. The leading explanation is that a small asteroid or comet fragment exploded in the atmosphere. The event shows how an object from space can cause enormous damage without leaving a typical crater.", "Tunguska event Siberia;Siberian taiga forest;meteor atmospheric explosion"),
("The Dancing Plague of 1518", "In Strasbourg in 1518, historical accounts describe people dancing uncontrollably for days. Historians still debate what caused the episode, with explanations ranging from social stress to illness. Popular retellings sometimes exaggerate details, so surviving records should be treated carefully rather than as a fully solved medical mystery.", "Strasbourg 16th century engraving;medieval dancing plague illustration;Holy Roman Empire historical map"),
("The Phaistos Disc", "Found on Crete in 1908, the Phaistos Disc carries symbols pressed into clay in a spiral pattern. Its signs have not been convincingly deciphered. Some researchers question whether it represents a writing system at all. Until more comparable evidence is found, the disc remains one of archaeology's most intriguing puzzles.", "Phaistos Disc;Minoan civilization Crete;ancient Crete archaeological site"),
("The Oak Island Mystery", "Oak Island in Nova Scotia has attracted treasure hunters for generations because of reports of unusual shafts and underground structures. Searches have uncovered artifacts, but a buried treasure has not been conclusively proven. The island's story shows how uncertain clues can inspire centuries of exploration and speculation.", "Oak Island Nova Scotia;Nova Scotia coastline;historic treasure map"),
("The Wow! Signal", "In 1977, a radio telescope in Ohio recorded a strong signal from space that lasted just over a minute. An astronomer circled the printout and wrote Wow! beside it. Follow-up searches never found a confirmed repeat. The signal remains unexplained, but there is no proof it came from extraterrestrial intelligence.", "Big Ear radio telescope;radio telescope observatory;star field"),
("The Lost Colony of Roanoke", "In 1587, English settlers established a colony on Roanoke Island. When a supply ship returned years later, the settlement was deserted. The word CROATOAN had been carved into a post, but no definitive record explains what happened. Some colonists may have joined nearby Indigenous communities. The settlement's fate remains uncertain.", "Roanoke Island historical map;historic map North Carolina;old sailing ship"),
("The London Beer Flood", "In 1814, a huge vat at a London brewery ruptured, releasing a wave of beer into nearby streets. The flood damaged homes and killed eight people. The event sounds unbelievable, but it was documented in contemporary reports. It was a strange historical disaster caused by industrial equipment failing.", "historic London brewery;19th century London street engraving;old beer brewery barrels"),
("The Bloop Sound", "In 1997, underwater microphones recorded an unusually powerful low-frequency sound in the Pacific. It inspired stories about unknown sea creatures. Later analysis suggested the sound was consistent with ice-related activity, such as cracking icebergs. It remains an example of how limited data can invite dramatic explanations.", "Antarctic iceberg;underwater hydrophone;Southern Ocean ice"),
]
day = datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).timetuple().tm_yday
title, video_script, image_searches = stories[(day - 1) % len(stories)]
with open(os.environ["GITHUB_ENV"], "a", encoding="utf-8") as env:
    env.write("STORY_TOPIC=" + title + "\n")
    env.write("IMAGE_SEARCHES=" + image_searches + "\n")
    env.write("VIDEO_SCRIPT<<STORY_SCRIPT_EOF\n")
    env.write(video_script + "\n")
    env.write("STORY_SCRIPT_EOF\n")
print("Today's topic: " + title)
print("Topic rotation: " + str((day - 1) % len(stories) + 1) + "/" + str(len(stories)))
